          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A peripheral blood smear shows normal-sized red blood cells with multiple ring-f</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A peripheral blood smear shows normal-sized red blood cells with multiple ring-form trophozoites in a single cell and banana-shaped gametocytes. Which Plasmodium species is responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Plasmodium vivax</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vivax enlarges red blood cells, produces only one ring per cell, and shows ameboid trophozoites with Schüffner's dots — never banana-shaped gametocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Plasmodium falciparum</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Normal-sized RBCs, multiple ring forms per cell, appliqué forms, and banana-shaped gametocytes are all hallmarks of P. falciparum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Plasmodium ovale</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ovale produces oval, fringed red blood cells with Schüffner's dots — it does not cause multiple rings per cell or banana-shaped gametocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Plasmodium malariae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Malariae is recognised by its characteristic band-form trophozoite stretching across the red blood cell, not by multiple rings or banana gametocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key clues are normal-sized RBCs (excludes vivax), multiple rings per cell, and banana-shaped gametocytes — all unique to P. falciparum. No other species produces banana-shaped gametocytes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The combination of normal-sized RBCs, multiple ring forms per cell, and banana-shaped gametocytes is pathognomonic for P. falciparum — the only species producing this distinctive gametocyte shape."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
