        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 303.725,
            "to": 313.725
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which stage of the Plasmodium life cycle is responsible for relapse in P. vivax </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which stage of the Plasmodium life cycle is responsible for relapse in P. vivax and P. ovale infections, months or years after the initial attack?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sporozoites in the mosquito salivary glands</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sporozoites initiate new infections when injected during a mosquito bite, but they do not persist dormant in the human host to cause relapse.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Merozoites released from ruptured schizonts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Merozoites drive the erythrocytic cycle and cause acute fever episodes, but they do not remain dormant in the liver to cause relapse.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypnozoites in hepatocytes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypnozoites are dormant liver-stage forms unique to P. vivax and P. ovale that reactivate months or years later, producing a fresh wave of merozoites and causing relapse.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gametocytes circulating in peripheral blood</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gametocytes are the sexual forms taken up by mosquitoes to continue the life cycle; they do not cause relapse in the human host.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Hypnozoites are dormant liver-stage parasites unique to P. vivax and P. ovale. They reactivate after the initial infection, releasing merozoites and causing relapse — which is why radical cure with primaquine is essential."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Hypnozoites are dormant hepatocyte forms unique to P. vivax and P. ovale. Their reactivation is why primaquine — the only anti-hypnozoite drug — is needed for radical cure."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
