          "label": "<p>A 35-year-old male from Odisha is diagnosed with P. vivax malaria. Before starti</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 35-year-old male from Odisha is diagnosed with P. vivax malaria. Before starting primaquine for radical cure, which investigation is mandatory?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Liver function tests</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While primaquine is hepatically metabolised, the critical pre-treatment risk is haemolytic anaemia from G6PD deficiency, not hepatotoxicity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemoglobin electrophoresis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemoglobin electrophoresis detects haemoglobinopathies such as sickle cell disease; it does not assess the risk of primaquine-induced oxidative haemolysis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum creatinine and blood urea</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Renal function monitoring is relevant in severe falciparum malaria but is not the mandatory pre-primaquine screening investigation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase assay</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>G6PD deficiency is common across India, especially in males. Primaquine causes severe haemolytic anaemia in deficient patients, making this test mandatory before prescribing.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Primaquine eliminates dormant hypnozoites but causes severe haemolytic anaemia in patients with G6PD deficiency. This enzyme assay is mandatory before starting the drug — deficient patients need a modified low-dose regimen under supervision."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! G6PD deficiency is prevalent in India, and primaquine triggers oxidative haemolysis in deficient individuals. The rule is absolute — always test before prescribing."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
