          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>When investigating a patient for lymphatic filariasis caused by Wuchereria bancr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>When investigating a patient for lymphatic filariasis caused by Wuchereria bancrofti, why must peripheral blood be collected between 10 PM and 2 AM?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The Culex mosquito vector feeds at night, injecting fresh microfilariae into the bloodstream</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Culex mosquito transmits the parasite but does not inject microfilariae — it picks them up during a blood meal. Nocturnal periodicity is an intrinsic parasite behaviour, not vector-driven.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Microfilariae exhibit nocturnal periodicity, appearing in peripheral blood only at night</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Wuchereria bancrofti microfilariae migrate to the peripheral circulation at night, peaking between 10 PM and 2 AM. A daytime blood sample will miss them entirely.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Eosinophil counts peak at night, making the smear easier to interpret</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While eosinophilia may accompany filarial infection, the timing of blood collection is dictated by the parasite's nocturnal periodicity, not by eosinophil fluctuations.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Giemsa staining of microfilariae requires freshly drawn blood processed within minutes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Giemsa staining works on blood drawn at any time of day. The constraint is that microfilariae are present in peripheral blood only during their nocturnal peak.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Wuchereria bancrofti microfilariae exhibit nocturnal periodicity, appearing in peripheral circulation only at night (peak: 10 PM–2 AM). This is an intrinsic parasite behaviour — daytime blood samples will be falsely negative."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Wuchereria bancrofti microfilariae exhibit nocturnal periodicity — they appear in peripheral blood only at night, peaking between 10 PM and 2 AM. Blood drawn outside this window will miss the diagnosis."
                }
              ],
